@@s025 What the exam is like inside
@@s026 Structure stable from 2018 to 2023. There are <b>5 problems</b> in Kattis style (text, input, output, examples) and <b>6 questions</b> that ask you to recognise the paradigm and to defend the choice with a short, precise solution.
@@s027 question
@@s028 what it asks
@@s029 typical points
@@s030 Gecko / counterexample
@@s031 a greedy proposed by 'Professor Gecko' is wrong: give a counterexample
@@s032 Greedy
@@s033 which problem, pseudocode with the explicit order, time
@@s034 Graph traversal
@@s035 which problem, how the graph is (nodes, arcs, directed, weights), which algorithm from which node, time
@@s036 Dynamic programming
@@s037 which problem, OPT with parameters and range, recurrence and base cases, which value is the answer, time and space
@@s038 Flow
@@s039 which problem, drawing of the network for Sample Input 1, meaning of the max flow, algorithm and time
@@s040 NP-hard
@@s041 which P1, which known P2, direction P2 ≤p P1, reduction in general and a small example
@@s042 What the teacher wants to read
@@s043 <b>The book is admitted, and must be used.</b> The teacher wants "I will use Dijkstra's algorithm [KT, p. …] to find …", not a reinvented algorithm.
@@s044 <b>Half a page per question.</b> Short, correct answers beat long ones. Illegible handwriting is read against you.
@@s045 <b>Parameters of the problem, not a generic <code>n</code>.</b> The time goes in the names of the text (N, K, R, T, d…). If you build a graph, say how many nodes and arcs it has as a function of those names.
@@s046 <b>Never "sort the input".</b> Write what you sort and in which direction: <i>"in increasing order of number of 1s"</i>.
@@s047 <b>Opening phrase for reductions:</b> <i>"Given an instance of BLABLA, we construct an instance of BLUB as follows."</i>
@@s048 <b>Draw the Sample Input 1</b> when the question asks for it (graph or flow network): it is the fastest way to show that the model is right.
@@s049 The time
@@s050 240 minutes for the exam. Initial reading and final re-reading of about 15 minutes each. The rest is divided between the problems and the questions: a 5-point question takes about 30–35 minutes. If a question gets stuck, write what you have and move on to the next one: a partial answer is worth more than nothing.
@@s051 Before handing in
@@s052 I wrote which problem (question (a) is worth a point of its own).
@@s053 <b>Greedy:</b> explicit order with direction, admissibility test, time.
@@s054 <b>Graph:</b> nodes, arcs, how many, directed, weights; algorithm with name and starting node; what I print.
@@s055 <b>DP:</b> meaning of OPT, range of the parameters, base cases, −∞ for the impossible, which cell is the answer, time and space.
@@s056 <b>Flow:</b> network for Sample Input 1, capacities, meaning and value of the max flow, algorithm and time.
@@s057 <b>NP-hard:</b> P2 ≤p P1 in the right direction, "Given an instance…", both directions, complete small example.
@@s058 Every time is in the parameters of the text, not with a generic <code>n</code>.
